      "side": 1,
      "message": "This catches the short write, but only after the data has been written. In the boundary case from the commit message (offset + fru.size() past the end of the EEPROM) the first bytes have already been overwritten, so the device is left with a truncated FRU while the error is reported. writeFRU() also throws here, so no rescan runs and the published FRU keeps showing the old contents.\n\nCould we reject up front when offset + fru.size() exceeds the EEPROM size (e.g. via fstat on the sysfs file) so a too-large FRU never touches the device? If that isn\u0027t feasible, is a rescan after a short write worth doing so the published data matches the device?",
      "revId": "713518c44ca80dc13aaef2a6ae4501115584baff",
      "serverId": "adbd0a64-1f21-4d83-b585-671fe73cb6e4"
    },
